From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Youll <adyoull@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:34:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] RISC OS: present only what changed

Converting the 8-bit game screen into the window's 32-bit surface, and
plotting it, was the biggest cost per frame on RISC OS (more than the
game's own drawing), and the palette cycling (water, lava: 17 colours
every 220 ms) made every cycle convert and plot the whole screen.

The new present path (riscos_present.h) keeps a copy of the last
presented game screen. In the area the game reports as changed, only
pixels that differ from the copy are converted, and only the 64-pixel
cells of 16-row bands that changed are plotted (up to 48 rectangles,
otherwise one around them all). When the palette changes, only pixels
using the changed colours (and any other differences) are redrawn,
instead of the whole frame; Display::_renderFrame no longer forces a
full frame for it on RISC OS. Scaled rows are converted once into a
buffer and copied, never read back from the surface (in full screen it
is the screen memory). The black borders are filled only when the size
changes. In full screen everything is redrawn every 5 seconds, in case
another program drew over the screen.

With a log (fheroes2$Log), the time spent converting and plotting, and
the pixels and rectangles per frame, are logged every 10 seconds.
